<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sojourner Truth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1797–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Isabella Baumfree</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sojourner Truth was an American abolitionist and campaigner for women's rights, born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> as Isabella Baumfree. She escaped with her infant daughter in 1826, a year before New York ended slavery, and later won a court case to recover her son, sold illegally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. Taking her new name in 1843, she became a travelling preacher and speaker against slavery, helped recruit Black soldiers for the Union during the Civil War, and campaigned for land and rights for freed people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sojourner-truth.jpg" alt="Sojourner Truth" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sojourner Truth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sojourner_Truth_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…who escaped from slavery in Maryland in 1838, became one of the movement’s most famous orators and founded his own newspaper; Harriet Tubman, who escaped in 1849, went back some thirteen times to lead about seventy people to freedom; <b>Sojourner Truth</b> preached against slavery across the northern states.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sojourner Truth</b>, who had freed herself from slavery, spoke at a women’s rights convention in Akron, Ohio, in 1851; the famous version of her speech, with its refrain “Ain’t I a woman?”, was published by Frances Dana Gage only in 1863 and differs from reports made at the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>…President's Proclamation&quot; for the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863; Captain Lindley Miller wrote, or wrote down, the &quot;Song of the First of Arkansas&quot; in 1864, a similar text being attributed to <b>Sojourner Truth</b>; German veterans of the revolutions of 1848–49 serving in the Blenker Division sang their own &quot;Kriegslied&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Battle Creek, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.31,-85.2&amp;year=1852" aria-label="Battle Creek on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2483 659 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sojourner Truth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2933 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2933 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.31,-85.2&amp;year=1852">What was here in 1852? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the rich rob the poor, and the poor rob one another.&#8221;</p><footer>The Narrative of Sojourner Truth (1850) &#183; original &#183; her words as recorded by Olive Gilbert, who wrote the narrative from her telling &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1674/pg1674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No, I did not run away; I walked away by day-light, and all because you had promised me a year of my time.&#8221;</p><footer>The Narrative of Sojourner Truth (1850), to her former master on leaving him &#183; original &#183; her words as recorded by Olive Gilbert, who wrote the narrative from her telling &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1674/pg1674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Of that I only know as I saw. I did not see him to be God; else, how could he stand between me and God? I saw him as a friend, standing between me and God, through whom, love flowed as from a fountain.&#8221;</p><footer>The Narrative of Sojourner Truth (1850), on her vision of Jesus &#183; original &#183; her words as recorded by Olive Gilbert, who wrote the narrative from her telling &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1674/pg1674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if the Lord comes and burns-as you say he will-I am not going away; I am going to stay here and stand the fire, like Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego!&#8221;</p><footer>The Narrative of Sojourner Truth (1850), to the Millerites at a camp meeting &#183; original &#183; as told to Olive Gilbert, who wrote the Narrative &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1674/pg1674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Lord only knows how many times I let my children go hungry, rather than take secretly the bread I liked not to ask for.&#8221;</p><footer>The Narrative of Sojourner Truth (1850), on her years in slavery &#183; original &#183; as told to Olive Gilbert, who wrote the Narrative &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1674/pg1674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
